WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.340
CUBRID,

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:03.391
in Python.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.036
From the driver to the
ORM, AI and examples.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:17.587
Open-source Python tools from
CUBRID Lab, all MIT-licensed.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.540
One: pycubrid.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.603
One line to install: a pure-Python
driver, no C extensions, no compiling.

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:40.153
Connect, open a cursor, execute:
the familiar DB-API 2.0 way.

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:50.159
asyncio, TLS encryption and
LOBs are supported too.

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:55.888
No C compiler. Anywhere Python runs.

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.250
Two: sqlalchemy-cubrid.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:07.865
Install it with pycubrid and
connect with one engine URL.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.924
Create tables, then save and query
rows with SQLAlchemy 2.0-style models.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.840
Alembic migrations, schema
reflection and an async engine.

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.130
Three: cubrid-mcp-server.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:46.033
An AI agent connects to CUBRID
over MCP and explores the schema.

00:01:50.200 --> 00:01:56.344
The agent turns a question into SQL;
the server runs read queries only.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:04.958
Read-only by default.
Writes and DDL are rejected,

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:08.479
and the write tool isn't even listed.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.205
Writes open only when an operator
enables them, per connection.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:23.104
Four: cubrid-cookbook-python.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:29.824
Start the FastAPI example with
Docker and check it in the browser.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:41.488
Flask, Django, dashboards,
AI agents and a Java migration guide.

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.207
CUBRID from Python, end to end.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:51.304
Get started at github.com/cubrid-lab.
